{
  "dom_id": "step-q50189",
  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/35f52e6a2c3eafe59fea3c14351067fdf4b78b8c2f247454429952eb3a6f8270.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{\u2032}=2xy$\n- ${y}^{\u2032}=-xy$\n- ${y}^{\u2032}=x+1$",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/e3afa2080ea81f679c9c9b3423cfbe7b11bed5625854a0a33ff01312ca3629e4.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=0$ and $y=0,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\n- Second, the lines $x=0$ and $y=0$ divide the plane into $4$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $4$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| $y>0$ | $\u2197$\n\n$(+)$ | $\u2198$\n\n$(-)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<0$ | $\u2198$\n\n$(-)$ | $\u2197$\n\n$(+)$ |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation I does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{\u2032}=2xy$ evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $y=0,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y>0$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<0$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |\n\n- Equation II gives the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{\u2032}=-xy$ evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $y=0,$ and the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y>0$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<0$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |\n\n- Equation III does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{\u2032}=x+1$ evaluates to zero at $x=-1$ only.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
